  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unusually strong upper level trough will\npersist over the Eastern CONUS through the weekend bringing a\nmuch colder airmass to the region with temperatures well below\nnormal. The initial cold front pushed offshore last evening and\nCAA will continue to ramp up overnight with temps in the low to\nmid 40s inland and around 50 coast by daybreak. It will be\nnoticeable cooler today with highs around 60. \n\nAnother strong shortwave and attendant cold front will push\nthrough late today and tonight bringing another surge of NW\nwinds. Guidance is suggest sufficient forcing and just enough\nmoisture remaining in the lower levels for a few showers to \ndevelop during the evening hours, similar to last evening.\nRainfall amounts will be nominal with only a few hundredths at\nbest. CAA will continue Friday night with lows dropping into the\nmid 30s inland to mid 40s coast. Current thinking is that there\nwill be enough of a breeze to limit the frost potential tonight \nand will not issue a Frost Advisory at this time. Temps on \nSaturday will be well below normal with highs in the mid 50s.\n\nProbabilities are higher for frost/freeze conditions away from \nthe waters Saturday Sunday nights as high pressure builds in \nand winds begin to diminish. Many areas are forecast to drop \ninto the low to mid 30s with coldest conditions expected along \nand west of highway 17 and have issued a Freeze Watch for this \narea for Saturday night. There is a possibility for a Frost \nAdvisory for several zones to the east of highway 17 but will\nlet future shifts evaluate as we get closer to the event. \n\nMuch below normal temps continue through early next week with\nhighs in the 50s to around 60 and lows in the 30s inland to 40s\ncoast. A gradual warming trend will occur mid to late next \nweek.",
